============================================================================================================
VERDICT
============================================================================================================
  ANSWER: YES — the clause would change verdicts.  Across the 12 grid points (476 (panel, null, rule) scorings), 32 clear the record's naive
  'gain > 0' bar and 18 clear their own count-matched bar; 14 published-by-the-naive-bar gains FLIP to non-findings
  (0.4375 of them).
  Chance predicts 0.05 x 476 = 23.8 survivals; observed 18 (ratio 0.76) — AT OR BELOW CHANCE.
  Under NL_PERM, which matches the move count AND the destinations and randomises
  only the timing, 0 of 8 naive passes survive.
  The 18 survivals collapse to 1 DISTINCT book(s); the rest
  is one path published under several threshold labels.
  CENSUS: of 746 C_STRICT committed chooser claims, 147 (0.1971) state a
  move count and 9 (0.0121) state a COUNT-MATCHED null.
  rho(move count, OOS Sharpe gain) = -0.6108 on the rule population built here.
  KEEP paths: 4b passes at 3 of 15 scored curves; 4a at 0 of 15.
  VERDICT: KILL (capital) / ANSWERED = YES.  No rule in this population is a tradable
  improvement on holding the anchor once its own move count is priced, which is
  exactly the finding: the queue's proposed PROTOCOL clause is supported, and it is a
  REPORTING clause, not a rule that earns capital.

GATES
  [PASS] G1 fast runner == engine.backtest on RULES v2 (U56): 1.388e-17 (target < 1e-12)
  [PASS] G2 the anchor book is identical wherever it appears in a ladder: 0.000e+00 (target < 1e-12)
  [PASS] G3 sums-based Sharpe == direct Sharpe on stitched paths: 6.661e-16 (target < 1e-10)

